  "_commit": {
    "id": "9831356494cf3096773098dea7216393db07dffb",
    "author": "feargalr <feargalr@gmail.com>",
    "committer": "feargalr <feargalr@gmail.com>",
    "message": "ssTaxSEA(): add a top-k reference and a proportion scale (v1.5.8)\n\nThe standard CLR centers each sample on the mean log value of every\ntaxon, zeros included. When many rare taxa drop out, as after\nchemotherapy, that reference falls and every surviving taxon's score\nrises, so hundreds of overlapping sets increase together with no change\namong the survivors. Adjusting for richness downstream does not remove\nit, and the reference also moves whenever rows are filtered differently.\n\nreference = \"top_k\" centers each sample on its top_k (default 20) most\nabundant detected taxa instead, capped at the number detected so zeros\nnever enter it. Rare taxa are still scored but no longer move everyone\nelse's score. K = 20 had the best worst case when known perturbations\n(rare-taxon loss, a bloom, losing the dominant taxon, lower depth) were\napplied to 2,071 curatedMetagenomicData samples across 85 studies.\n\nscale = \"proportion\" divides each sample by its total and applies the\npseudocount as a proportion (default 1e-6), so counts, proportions and\npercentages give the same scores and the pseudocount does not vary with\ndepth.\n\nPercentages were previously accepted on the count scale, where the 0.5\npseudocount (half a percent) swamped every rarer taxon. They are now\nrefused with a pointer to scale = \"proportion\". Defaults are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n",
    "time": 1792996675
  },
